VERDICT: PASS
====================================================================================

  The target is the pinned tree, no smoke override is present, and every
  path value is ordinary text. A caller may invoke the runner.

  RESIDUAL, stated because none of it can be closed from a clone. Every file
  named below is REFERENCE under AAP 0.6.1/0.6.5, so each defect is reported
  with the exact patch provisioning must apply, and each is carried in
  oss-scan-results/run-record.md. The gate above is the compensating control
  for all three, and it compensates only for callers that read its status:
  each harness/bin/run-<tool>.sh remains executable in its own right, and an
  invocation that does not read this exit status is not bound by it.

    1. harness/bin/run-trivy.sh lines 52-53 -- CWE-94. Both lines build a
       python3 -c program by interpolating $TRIVY_CACHE_DIR into its source
       text, so a cache directory whose literal name closes the embedded
       string executes arbitrary Python with the process's ambient
       environment. Proven exploitable at this checkpoint: a crafted value
       created a marker file while the runner still exited 0 and wrote a valid
       artifact, so the compromise leaves no failure signal.
       PATCH: pass the path as positional argv to fixed code instead of
       interpolating environment text into program source --
       python3 -c 'import json,sys;d=json.load(open(sys.argv[1]));
       print("v%s UpdatedAt=%s"%(d["Version"],d["UpdatedAt"]))'
       "$TRIVY_CACHE_DIR/db/metadata.json" -- and the same for java-db on :53.
       Mitigated here by the path-text policy above, which refuses every
       shell- and source-significant character in $TRIVY_CACHE_DIR before any
       runner is invoked, and which names that variable in its own diagnostic.

    2. harness/env.sh line 91 -- the missing-tree halt AAP 0.8.1 mandates is
       dead. `mkdir -p "$HARNESS_RAW_DIR" "$HARNESS_LOG_DIR"
       "$HARNESS_SCRATCH_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true` runs on every runner's
       source of env.sh, so scope.sh lines 68-69's correct `-d` guard is
       handed trees that were created on the way to it; and `|| true` also
       swallows a permission failure, making a tree that could not be created
       indistinguishable from one that was.
       PATCH: drop the two artifact trees from the mkdir -p, keeping only
       HARNESS_SCRATCH_DIR (which this run legitimately owns and creates), and
       drop the `|| true`, so a failure to create the scratch directory is
       reported rather than hidden: `mkdir -p "$HARNESS_SCRATCH_DIR"`.
       Provisioning leaves raw/ and logs/ created and empty; this run neither
       creates nor clears them. Mitigated here by the artifact-tree checks
       above, which refuse an absent or non-directory tree before any runner.

    3. harness/env.sh lines 19-22 -- both preflight gates' documented override
       is destroyed by sourcing the environment. HARNESS_DIR and
       HARNESS_REPO_ROOT are assigned UNCONDITIONALLY while 27 other exports
       use ${VAR:-default}, so a caller who exports HARNESS_REPO_ROOT to point
       a gate at a specific checkout has it silently overwritten the moment
       env.sh is sourced -- which is what every runner and every documented
       shell does. Both gates implement and document that override
       (preflight_scan_target.py repo_root, preflight_graph_identity.py
       repo_root), and it works only in a shell that has NOT sourced env.sh.
       PATCH: `export HARNESS_DIR="${HARNESS_DIR:-$(cd "$(dirname
       "$_harness_self")" && pwd)}"` and the same shape for
       HARNESS_REPO_ROOT, matching the other 27 exports.
